.TH TANDEM 1
.SH NAME
tandem \- two players, one screen, one of them somewhere else
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B games/tandem
.br
.B games/tandem -j
.I addr
.br
.B games/tandem -serve
.br
.B games/tandem
[
.B -t
|
.B -b
]
.PP
.B "styxlisten -A 'tcp!*!" port "' games/tandem -serve"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Tandem
is an arena of girders with coins on it and two players in it.
Running along collects coins; jumping up into the underside of a girder
knocks whoever is standing on it off their feet, which is the only way
the two players touch and is both the help and the hindrance.
The world wraps left to right.
After a minute the higher score wins.
.PP
Arrow keys or
.B wasd
move,
.BR x ,
.B .
or space jumps,
.B q
or Escape quits.
.SS The game is a file server
.I Tandem
in its playing form has a window and a keyboard.
The game does not.
.B "tandem -serve"
has neither: it serves Styx on its standard input and output, and what
it serves is
.TP
.B ctl
written to:
.B join
asks for a player number,
.B reset
starts the round again,
.B drive
takes the clock,
.B step
moves the game on one frame
.TP
.B pad
written to:
.BI "" N " MASK" ,
meaning player
.I N
is holding
.I MASK
.TP
.B state
read: where everything is, as text
.TP
.B status
read: the answer to the last write
.PP
A player is whatever is writing to
.BR pad .
Running
.I tandem
with no arguments starts a server down a pipe and mounts it; running it
with
.BI -j " addr"
mounts a game somewhere else.
From that point the two are the same code, because mounting a pipe and
mounting a TCP connection are the same thing.
That is the whole argument, and it is why there are no separate host and
client programs here.
.PP
.B State
is text so that a person can read it while the game is running, and
because the first thing anybody does with a protocol they cannot read is
guess about it.
A frame is:
.IP
.EX
frame N OVER
p 1 X Y FLAGS SCORE
p 2 X Y FLAGS SCORE
c K TX TY LIVE
.EE
.PP
.I Flags
are 1 on the ground, 2 knocked over, 4 facing left, 8 joined.
.SS Playing across machines
On one:
.IP
.EX
styxlisten -A 'tcp!*!6789' games/tandem -serve
.EE
.PP
On the other:
.IP
.EX
games/tandem -j tcp!thathost!6789
.EE
.PP
Both players are then clients of the same game, and neither is
simulating anything.
.B /tests/tandemnet.sh
does exactly this to a local port and runs the self test through it.
.PP
Nothing falls back to anything.
A client that cannot mount does not play and says so, and a client whose
read of
.B state
fails stops rather than drawing the last thing it saw \- which would
look exactly like still being connected.
.SS What it costs
.B -b
measures a frame over the mount.
Over a pipe on an Apple M-series host, two thousand of each:
.IP
.EX
                      opened each    held open
write pad                 867.0 us      268.5 us
read state               1263.5 us      641.0 us
both, as a frame         3083.0 us     1278.0 us
.EE
.PP
A frame at 60Hz has 16666 microseconds, so a frame over the mount is
about
.B 8%
of the budget \- if the two files a client touches every frame are
opened once and kept.
Opening them each frame costs a walk, an open and a clunk on top of the
read or write, which is four Styx round trips where one would do, and
takes 19% of the budget instead.
.SS The clock
The game keeps the time, so that two clients on two machines cannot
disagree about what frame it is, and it keeps it by catching up to the
wall clock when somebody reads
.B state
rather than by running a process of its own.
A client that writes
.B drive
takes the clock instead and the game then moves only when
.B step
is written, which is what makes
.B -t
reproducible.
